@end menu

@node	3.1.1, 3.1.2, 3.1, 3.1
@subsection Local State Variables

To illustrate what we mean by having a computational object with time-varying
state, let us model the situation of withdrawing money from a bank account.  We
will do this using a function @code{withdraw}, which takes as argument an
@code{amount} to be withdrawn.  If there is enough money in the account to
accommodate the withdrawal, then @code{withdraw} should return the balance
remaining after the withdrawal.  Otherwise, @code{withdraw} should return the
message @emph{Insufficient funds}. For example, if we begin with $100 in the
account, we should obtain the following sequence of responses using
@code{withdraw}:

@example rust
let balance = Cell::new(100);

let withdraw = |amount: i128| @{
    if balance.get() >= amount @{
        balance.set(balance.get() - amount);
        Reply::Balance(balance.get())
    @} else @{
        Reply::Message("Insufficient funds")
    @}
@};

withdraw(25)
// => 75

withdraw(25)
// => 50

withdraw(60)
// => "Insufficient funds"

withdraw(15)
// => 35
@end example

@noindent
Observe that the expression @code{withdraw(25)}, evaluated twice, yields
different values.  This is a new kind of behavior for a procedure.  Until now,
all our procedures could be viewed as specifications for computing mathematical
functions.  A call to a procedure computed the value of the function applied to
the given arguments, and two calls to the same procedure with the same
arguments always produced the same result.@footnote{Actually, this is not quite
true.  One exception was the random-number generator in @ref{1.2.6}.
Another exception involved the operation/type tables we introduced in
@ref{2.4.3}, where the values of two calls to @code{get} with the same
arguments depended on intervening calls to @code{put}.  On the other hand,
until we introduce assignment, we have no way to create such procedures
ourselves.}

To implement @code{withdraw}, we use a @code{Cell} holding the @code{balance}
of money in the account and define @code{withdraw} as the closure above, which
reaches that cell every time it runs.  The @code{withdraw} closure checks to
see if @code{balance} is at least as large as the requested @code{amount}.  If
so, @code{withdraw} decrements @code{balance} by @code{amount} and returns the
new value of @code{balance}.  Otherwise, @code{withdraw} returns the
@emph{Insufficient funds} message.  Decrementing @code{balance} is accomplished
by the statement

@example rust
balance.set(balance.get() - amount);
@end example

@noindent
This is an assignment to state held in a @code{Cell}, this edition's
@newterm{interior mutability}: the cell's contents change while the cell itself
is only ever borrowed.  The general form of the book's @code{set!} has two
spellings here:

@example rust
@code{⟨}@var{name}@code{⟩} = @code{⟨}@var{new-value}@code{⟩};
@code{⟨}@var{cell}@code{⟩}.set(@code{⟨}@var{new-value}@code{⟩});
@end example

@noindent
Here @code{⟨}@var{name}@code{⟩} is a @code{mut} binding and
@code{⟨}@var{cell}@code{⟩} is a cell whose contents may change.  Each changes
its target so that its value is the result obtained by evaluating
@code{⟨}@var{new-value}@code{⟩}.  In the case at hand, we are changing
@code{balance} so that its new value will be the result of subtracting
@code{amount} from the previous value of @code{balance}.@footnote{Assignment
in Rust evaluates to the unit value @code{()}, which carries no information;
like the book's @code{set!}, it should be used only for its effect, not for
its value.

The book's @code{set!} reflects a Scheme naming convention in which operations
that change the values of variables (or that change data structures, as we will
see in @ref{3.3}) are given names that end with an exclamation point.  Rust
marks the mutation in the type instead of the name: a plain binding must
declare @code{mut} to be assignable, and interior state must sit behind a
@code{Cell} or a @code{RefCell}.  Predicates in Rust are conventionally named
to begin with @code{is_}, in the role of Scheme's question mark.}

@code{Withdraw} also relies on sequencing to first decrement @code{balance}
and then return the value of @code{balance}.  In general, evaluating the block

@example rust
@{
    @code{⟨}@var{statement-1}@code{⟩};
    @code{⟨}@var{statement-2}@code{⟩};
    @r{…}
    @code{⟨}@var{expression-k}@code{⟩}
@}
@end example

@noindent
causes the statements @math{{⟨statement_1⟩}} through @math{{⟨statement_{k-1}⟩}}
to be executed in sequence and the value of the final expression
@math{{⟨exp_k⟩}} to be the value of the entire block.@footnote{Rust needs no
separate sequencing form, where the book's Scheme reaches for @code{begin}:
the body of a function is always a block, and so is each arm of a
@code{match}.  Where the book writes @code{begin}, this edition simply writes
the statements in order and lets the block's final expression be the answer.}

Although @code{withdraw} works as desired, the variable @code{balance}
presents a problem.  As specified above, @code{balance} is a name in the
enclosing scope and is freely accessible to be examined or modified by any
later code that can name it: one call to @code{balance.set} is all it takes to
reach past @code{withdraw} and change the account.  It would be much better if
we could somehow make @code{balance} internal to @code{withdraw}, so that
@code{withdraw} would be the only procedure that could access @code{balance}
directly and any other procedure could access @code{balance} only indirectly
(through calls to @code{withdraw}).  This would more accurately model the
notion that @code{balance} is a local state variable used by @code{withdraw}
to keep track of the state of the account.

We can make @code{balance} internal to @code{withdraw} by rewriting the
definition as follows:

@example rust
pub fn new_withdraw() -> impl FnMut(i128) -> Reply @{
    let balance = Cell::new(100);
    move |amount| @{
        if balance.get() >= amount @{
            balance.set(balance.get() - amount);
            Reply::Balance(balance.get())
        @} else @{
            Reply::Message("Insufficient funds")
        @}
    @}
@}
@end example

@noindent
What we have done here is bind a local variable @code{balance}, initialized to
100, inside the body of @code{new_withdraw}, and then use @code{move} to hand
the returned closure its own ownership of that cell.  Because the closure is
an @code{FnMut}, it may write the cell it captured on every call.  This
closure---returned as the result of calling @code{new_withdraw}---behaves in
precisely the same way as @code{withdraw} but whose variable @code{balance} is
not accessible by any other procedure.@footnote{In programming-language
jargon, the variable @code{balance} is said to be @newterm{encapsulated} within
the closure that @code{new_withdraw} returns.  Encapsulation reflects the
general system-design principle known as the @newterm{hiding principle}: One
can make a system more modular and robust by protecting parts of the system
from each other; that is, by providing information access only to those parts
of the system that have a ``need to know.''}

Combining assignment with local variables is the general programming technique
we will use for constructing computational objects with local state.
Unfortunately, using this technique raises a serious problem: procedures with
local state are no longer specifications for computing mathematical functions,
so we cannot understand them by the substitution-style reasoning of
@ref{1.1.5}.  As a consequence, we technically have at this point no way to
understand why the closure returned by @code{new_withdraw} behaves as claimed
above.  To really understand a procedure such as @code{new_withdraw},
we will need a new model of procedure application.  In @ref{3.2} we will
develop such a model, together with an explanation of assignment and local
variables in terms of what a closure captures and who owns it.  First, however,
we examine some variations on the theme established by @code{new_withdraw}.

The following function, @code{make_withdraw}, creates ``withdrawal
processors.''  The formal parameter @code{balance} in @code{make_withdraw}
specifies the initial amount of money in the account.@footnote{In contrast with
@code{new_withdraw} above, we do not have to bind a @code{Cell} to make
@code{balance} a local variable, since formal parameters are already local:
the @code{move} closure takes ownership of the parameter itself.  This will be
clearer after the discussion of the environment model of evaluation in
@ref{3.2}.  (See also @ref{Exercise 3.10}.)}

@example rust
pub fn make_withdraw(mut balance: i128) -> impl FnMut(i128) -> Reply @{
    move |amount| @{
        if balance >= amount @{
            balance -= amount;
            Reply::Balance(balance)
        @} else @{
            Reply::Message("Insufficient funds")
        @}
    @}
@}
@end example

@noindent
@code{Make_withdraw} can be used as follows to create two objects @code{w1}
and @code{w2}:

@example rust
let mut w1 = make_withdraw(100);
let mut w2 = make_withdraw(100);

w1(50)
// => 50

w2(70)
// => 30

w2(40)
// => "Insufficient funds"

w1(40)
// => 10
@end example

@noindent
Observe that @code{w1} and @code{w2} are completely independent objects, each
with its own local state variable @code{balance}.  Withdrawals from one do not
affect the other.

We can also create objects that handle deposits as well as withdrawals, and
thus we can represent simple bank accounts.  Here is the definition that
returns a ``bank-account object'' with a specified initial balance:

@example rust
pub struct Account @{
    balance: Rc<Cell<i128>>,
@}

pub fn make_account(balance: i128) -> Account @{
    Account @{
        balance: Rc::new(Cell::new(balance)),
    @}
@}

impl Account @{
    /// Sends the account a message and returns its answer.
    pub fn send(&self, request: Request) -> Reply @{
        match request @{
            Request::Withdraw(amount) => @{
                if self.balance.get() >= amount @{
                    self.balance.set(self.balance.get() - amount);
                    Reply::Balance(self.balance.get())
                @} else @{
                    Reply::Message("Insufficient funds")
                @}
            @}
            Request::Deposit(amount) => @{
                self.balance.set(self.balance.get() + amount);
                Reply::Balance(self.balance.get())
            @}
        @}
    @}
@}
@end example

@noindent
Each call to @code{make_account} sets up an object with a local state variable
@code{balance}, held in a reference-counted cell so that the object can be
named more than once (a need that will arise in @ref{3.1.3} and in
@ref{Exercise 3.7}).  Within the object, @code{send} dispatches on the
message it is given and answers with the new balance or with the
@emph{Insufficient funds} message.  The @code{Account} type is this edition's
@newterm{shared ownership} made concrete: the count in the @code{Rc} is what
lets two names hold one balance, while the @code{Cell} is what lets the
balance change under a shared name.  This is precisely the
@newterm{message-passing} style of programming that we saw in @ref{2.4.3},
although here we are using it in conjunction with the ability to modify local
variables.  Where the book's @code{dispatch} needs an error arm for an
unknown message, the @code{match} over @code{Request} needs none: the
compiler proves that every message is handled, because a message that is not
one of the two variants cannot be spelled.

@code{Make_account} can be used as follows:

@example rust
let acc = make_account(100);

acc.send(Request::Withdraw(50))
// => 50

acc.send(Request::Withdraw(60))
// => "Insufficient funds"

acc.send(Request::Deposit(40))
// => 90

acc.send(Request::Withdraw(60))
// => 30
@end example

@noindent
Each call to @code{acc.send} dispatches to the handling for the message it
carries, applied to the specified @code{amount}.  As was the case with
@code{make_withdraw}, another call to @code{make_account}

@example rust
let acc2 = make_account(100);
@end example

@noindent
will produce a completely separate account object, which maintains its own
local @code{balance}.

