@strong{@anchor{Exercise 3.1}Exercise 3.1:} An @newterm{accumulator} is a
function that is called repeatedly with a single numeric argument and
accumulates its arguments into a sum.  Each time it is called, it returns the
currently accumulated sum.  Write a function @code{make_accumulator} that
generates accumulators, each maintaining an independent sum.  The input to
@code{make_accumulator} should specify the initial value of the sum; for
example

@example rust
let mut a = make_accumulator(5);

a(10)
// => 15

a(10)
// => 25
@end example
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 3.2}Exercise 3.2:} In software-testing applications,
it is useful to be able to count the number of times a given procedure is
called during the course of a computation.  Write a function
@code{make_monitored} that takes as input a function, @code{f}, that itself
takes one @code{f64} input.  The result returned by @code{make_monitored} is a
third function, say @code{mf}, that keeps track of the number of times it has
been called by maintaining an internal counter.  The input to @code{mf} is a
request: if the request is the value @code{MonitorRequest::HowManyCalls},
then @code{mf} returns the value of the counter; if the request is
@code{MonitorRequest::ResetCount}, then @code{mf} resets the counter to zero;
and for any other request, @code{MonitorRequest::Call(x)}, @code{mf} returns
the result of calling @code{f} on @code{x} and increments the counter.  For
instance, we could make a monitored version of @code{sqrt}:

@example rust
let mut s = make_monitored(f64::sqrt);

s(MonitorRequest::Call(100.0))
// => MonitorReply::Result(10.0)

s(MonitorRequest::HowManyCalls)
// => MonitorReply::Count(1)
@end example
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 3.3}Exercise 3.3:} Modify the @code{make_account}
function so that it creates password-protected accounts.  That is,
@code{make_account} should take a password as an additional argument, as in

@example rust
